@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+# Self-hosting
+
+Fizzy is a Rails app, and this repository ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) config for running it on **one box**: a Linux server with Docker, one container running everything, SQLite for the database, background jobs inside that container, and SSL from the proxy.
+
+If you are looking for how 37signals runs [fizzy.do](https://fizzy.do), that's [`saas/config`](../saas/config) and it is not part of the open source deployment.
+
+## What you need
+
+- A Linux server with at least 2 GB of memory, with ports 80 and 443 reachable from the internet. Docker is installed for you by `bin/kamal setup` if it isn't there already.
+- SSH access to that server as root. If you use another user, set it in `config/deploy.yml` with `ssh: user: …`.
+- A DNS record for your hostname pointing at the server. kamal-proxy asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate for it, so it has to resolve before you deploy.
+- On your own machine: Ruby (see `.ruby-version`), git, and Docker, since the image is built locally. Plus a container registry you can push to, such as Docker Hub.
+
+## 1. Fork, clone, set up
+
+Fork the repository, clone your fork, and install the toolchain:
+
+```sh
+bin/setup
+```
+
+## 2. Fill in `config/deploy.yml`
+
+Everything you need to change is marked `FILL IN` in [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml):
+
+1. `host` — the hostname people will use, e.g. `fizzy.mycompany.com`.
+2. Your outgoing mail server — address, port, HELO domain and the From address.
+3. The Docker Hub repository to push the image to, in `image:` and `registry: username:`.
+
+## 3. Put your secrets in `.kamal/secrets`
+
+Create `.kamal/secrets` in the root of the repository. It is a dotenv-style file and it is git-ignored, so it stays on your machine:
+
+```sh
+# A token with push access to the registry, e.g. a Docker Hub access token
+KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=…
+
+# Signs cookies and links. Generate one with `bin/rails secret`.
+SECRET_KEY_BASE=…
+
+# Login for your mail server. If your relay needs no authentication, put
+# anything you like here.
+SMTP_USERNAME=…
+SMTP_PASSWORD=…
+```
+
+Nothing else has to be set. Browser push notifications stay off until you add `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` here and uncomment the two lines in the `env.secret` list in `config/deploy.yml` (see [Development](development.md#web-push-notifications) for how to generate a key pair).
+
+## 4. Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+`bin/kamal` is the Kamal from this repository's `Gemfile`, so it always matches `config/deploy.yml`. `kamal` works the same if you have it installed globally.
+
+This installs Docker on the box, builds the image, pushes it to your registry, boots kamal-proxy, and starts Fizzy. The first boot creates and migrates the SQLite databases, which live on a Docker volume, so they are still there for the next deploy.
+
+When the command finishes, your Fizzy is at `https://your-host`. Create the first account by signing up.
+
+## What runs where
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Web app, Solid Queue jobs, SQLite, uploads | one container, `fizzy-web` |
+| Database, cache, queue and uploaded files | the `fizzy` Docker volume, mounted at `/rails/storage` |
+| SSL, port 80/443, zero-downtime swaps | `kamal-proxy`, in its own container |
+
+Background jobs run in the web container: puma starts Solid Queue through its `plugin :solid_queue` (see `config/puma.rb`), so there is nothing else to keep alive. Recurring jobs are listed in `config/recurring.yml` — notifications, entropy postponements and cleanup.
+
+Outbound email goes through the mail server you configured, with the credentials from `.kamal/secrets`. Magic links, notifications and exports all arrive that way, so a mail server that works is what makes Fizzy usable.
+
+## Deploying changes
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+The image is rebuilt from your checkout, pushed, and swapped in without dropping requests. Pending migrations are applied by the container's entrypoint (`bin/docker-entrypoint`) before the server starts, so you don't need a separate step.
+
+To go back to the previous version: `bin/kamal rollback <version>`. Note that a migration that has already run isn't undone by this.
+
+## Looking around
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app logs          # container logs, -f to follow
+bin/kamal console           # a Rails console inside the running container
+bin/kamal ssh               # a shell in the same place
+bin/kamal proxy reboot      # if kamal-proxy itself gets wedged
+```
+
+## Backups
+
+Everything that matters is in the `fizzy` volume. To see where the box keeps it:
+
+```sh
+ssh root@your-host docker volume inspect fizzy
+```
+
+And to take a copy, from your own machine:
+
+```sh
+ssh root@your-host "docker run --rm -v fizzy:/data alpine tar cz -C /data ." > fizzy-$(date +%F).tar.gz
+ssh root@your-host "docker run --rm -i -v fizzy:/data alpine tar xz -C /data" < fizzy-$(date +%F).tar.gz # restore
+```
+
+## Variations
+
+- **A different registry.** Add `server:` to the `registry` block in `config/deploy.yml`, e.g. `ghcr.io`, and prefix the image name with it.
+- **An ARM box.** Change `builder: arch:` to `arm64`.
+- **Building on the box** rather than your own machine, if you'd rather not have Docker locally: set `builder: remote: ssh://root@your-host` and `local: false`. The box then needs Docker with the buildx plugin, which the Docker install script provides.
+- **Big mail relays, self-signed CAs, port 25.** `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_TLS`, `SMTP_STARTTLS_AUTO`, `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` and `SMTP_OPENSSL_VERIFY_MODE` are all read from the environment — see `config/environments/production.rb`.
+- **More accounts on the same Fizzy.** Signups close as soon as the first account exists. Set `MULTI_TENANT: "true"` in the `env.clear` list if you want to keep taking them.
+- **More than one box.** Then you want a real database server and a separate jobs role, which is beyond what this config is for. `saas/config/deploy.yml` shows the shape of it.
diff --git a/test/deploy_config_test.rb b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e4c74d5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "erb"
+require "yaml"
+require "json"
+require "open3"
+
+# The one-box Kamal config that a fork of Fizzy is meant to deploy with.
+class DeployConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  HOST = "fizzy.example.com"
+  MARKER = "FIZZY_DEPLOY_CONFIG_TEST:"
+
+  setup do
+    @config = yaml_config
+  end
+
+  test "deploys to one box" do
+    assert_equal "fizzy", @config["service"]
+    hosts = @config.dig("servers", "web", "hosts")
+    assert_not_nil hosts, "config/deploy.yml has no web hosts"
+    assert_equal 1, hosts.size, "This config is for a single box"
+  end
+
+  test "ssl comes from the proxy" do
+    assert_equal true, @config.dig("proxy", "ssl")
+    assert_equal @config.dig("servers", "web", "hosts").first, @config.dig("proxy", "host"),
+      "kamal-proxy needs the hostname it should get a certificate for"
+  end
+
+  test "data survives a deploy" do
+    volume = @config["volumes"]&.find { |v| v.end_with?("/rails/storage") }
+    assert_not_nil volume, "The SQLite databases and uploaded files have to live on a volume"
+    assert_predicate volume.split(":").first, :present?, "Expected a named volume, not a path on the host"
+  end
+
+  test "assets are bridged across deploys" do
+    assert_equal "/rails/public/assets", @config["asset_path"]
+  end
+
+  test "the app knows the host it is served from" do
+    assert_equal @config.dig("servers", "web", "hosts").first, @config.dig("env", "clear", "APP_HOST")
+  end
+
+  test "there is a mail server to fill in" do
+    clear = @config.dig("env", "clear")
+    assert_predicate clear["SMTP_ADDRESS"], :present?, "Fizzy sends magic links by email, so it needs an SMTP server"
+    assert_predicate clear["SMTP_PORT"], :present?
+    assert_predicate clear["SMTP_DOMAIN"], :present?
+    assert_predicate clear["MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS"], :present?
+
+    assert_includes @config.dig("env", "secret"), "SMTP_USERNAME"
+    assert_includes @config.dig("env", "secret"), "SMTP_PASSWORD"
+  end
+
+  test "secrets come from .kamal/secrets, not from this file" do
+    secrets = @config.dig("env", "secret")
+    assert_includes secrets, "SECRET_KEY_BASE", "Production has no credentials file, so it needs SECRET_KEY_BASE"
+    assert_empty secrets.grep(/PASSWORD.*=/), "Passwords belong in .kamal/secrets"
+
+    registry_password = @config.dig("registry", "password")
+    assert_kind_of Array, registry_password, "The registry password is read from a secret"
+    assert_predicate registry_password&.first, :present?
+  end
+
+  test "the image can be built" do
+    assert_predicate @config["image"], :present?
+    assert_predicate @config.dig("builder", "arch"), :present?, "Kamal needs to know which architecture to build for"
+  end
+
+  test "background jobs run on the box" do
+    puma = File.read(Rails.root.join("config/puma.rb"))
+    message = "Jobs are meant to run in the web container, through puma's solid_queue plugin"
+    assert_match(/^ *plugin :solid_queue$/, puma, message)
+    assert_predicate @config.dig("env", "clear", "JOB_CONCURRENCY"), :present?
+  end
+
+  test "production reads APP_HOST and the SMTP_ variables" do
+    settings = settings_from_production(<<~RUBY)
+      puts "#{MARKER}" + JSON.generate({
+        default_url_options: Rails.application.config.action_mailer.default_url_options,
+        root_url: Rails.application.routes.url_helpers.root_url,
+        delivery_method: Rails.application.config.action_mailer.delivery_method.to_s,
+        smtp: Rails.application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings&.transform_values(&:to_s)
+      })
+    RUBY
+
+    assert_equal({ "host" => HOST, "protocol" => "https" }, settings["default_url_options"])
+    assert_equal "https://#{HOST}/", settings["root_url"]
+    assert_equal "smtp", settings["delivery_method"]
+    assert_equal "smtp.example.com", settings.dig("smtp", "address")
+    assert_equal "465", settings.dig("smtp", "port")
+    assert_equal "true", settings.dig("smtp", "tls")
+    assert_equal "user@example.com", settings.dig("smtp", "user_name")
+    assert_equal "plain", settings.dig("smtp", "authentication")
+  end
+
+  test "production boots without APP_HOST and the SMTP_ variables" do
+    settings = settings_from_production(<<~RUBY, app_host: false, smtp_address: false)
+      puts "#{MARKER}" + JSON.generate({
+        default_url_options: Rails.application.config.action_mailer.default_url_options,
+        delivery_method: Rails.application.config.action_mailer.delivery_method.to_s,
+        smtp: Rails.application.config.action_mailer.smtp_settings
+      })
+    RUBY
+
+    assert_nil settings["default_url_options"]
+    assert_empty settings["delivery_method"]
+    assert_nil settings["smtp"]
+  end
+
+  private
+    def yaml_config
+      template = File.read(Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))
+      YAML.unsafe_load(ERB.new(template, trim_mode: "-").result)
+    end
+
+    def settings_from_production(code, app_host: true, smtp_address: true)
+      variables = {
+        "RAILS_ENV" => "production",
+        "SECRET_KEY_BASE" => "only-here-so-production-boots",
+        "SMTP_PORT" => "465",
+        "SMTP_TLS" => "true",
+        "SMTP_DOMAIN" => "example.com",
+        "SMTP_USERNAME" => "user@example.com",
+        "SMTP_PASSWORD" => "password"
+      }
+      variables["APP_HOST"] = HOST if app_host
+      variables["SMTP_ADDRESS"] = "smtp.example.com" if smtp_address
+
+      output, status = Open3.capture2e(variables, Rails.root.join("bin/rails").to_s, "runner", code, chdir: Rails.root)
+      assert_predicate status, :success?, "bin/rails runner failed in production:\n#{output}"
+
+      settings = output[/^#{MARKER}(.*)$/, 1]
+      raise "The production settings were not reported:\n#{output}" unless settings
+
+      JSON.parse(settings)
+    end
+end
